# Summary
Introduced: POST Object authorization now uses conditional header values for bucket policy evaluation without applying those conditions to the storage commit. Existing PUT Object keeps the condition in StreamingPutContext and passes it at finalization, but POST Object does not.
POST Object now parses If-Match/If-None-Match and forwards their values into the PutObjectPolicyContext used for bucket policy authorization. However, the parsed WriteCondition is not stored in StreamingPostContext and finalize_streaming_post_object always passes WriteCondition::default() to finalize_authorized_stream_put. As a result, a client can send If-None-Match: * or an arbitrary If-Match value to satisfy a bucket policy condition such as Deny when s3:if-none-match is null or not '*', but the actual object write will not enforce create-only or ETag matching semantics. This allows overwriting an existing object even when the bucket policy was intended to allow only conditional writes.

# Resolution
Reviewed against the AWS-facing oracle tests added in commit [67175e5](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/67175e5) (`Align POST object policy conditions with AWS`) and the related invalid finding in `security/codex-22d83bb`.

This finding is not valid for AWS compatibility. AWS exposes `POST Object` HTTP `If-Match` and `If-None-Match` headers to bucket-policy condition keys (`s3:if-match` and `s3:if-none-match`), but those headers are policy context only for `POST Object`; they are not enforced as write preconditions. Repeated `POST Object` requests with `If-None-Match: *` are expected to return 204 and overwrite the object, matching the current local behavior.

The practical policy outcome is that a bucket policy cannot make `POST Object` into a create-only conditional write using `s3:if-none-match`. If a deployment needs to enforce `If-None-Match` write preconditions, it must allow the object-write APIs that actually support conditional writes and deny/block `POST Object` for that path.

